description:
Goal: produce a concise, language-agnostic playbook and concrete snippets that show safe, ergonomic client patterns for: 1) incremental streaming responses, 2) tool/function invocation lifecycle, and 3) structured JSON-first outputs across TypeScript, Python, and Go. This ticket delivers: - A single-page developer playbook describing the invoker contract and edge cases (timeout, partial schemas, backpressure). - Minimal, copy-pasteable examples for TS/Py/Go that show the recommended wiring (stream handling, tool handshake, typed parsing). - A compatibility checklist for SDK maintainers (behavioral tests, semantic guarantees, error shapes). Motivation: we keep getting inconsistent behavior across language bindings when clients consume progressive outputs and invoke tools mid-stream. This causes support tickets and customer confusion when outputs arrive in chunks or when the server emits tool-calls after initial text tokens. Deliverables: 1) Playbook doc (Confluence link) summarizing the invoker contract and envelope v2. 2) Example package for each language in the repo under examples/interop-invoker. 3) Automated behavioral tests (integration harness) that assert:   - event ordering for chunked tokens vs tool events,   - idempotency on retry-able tool calls,   - structured output validity across incremental emissions. Approach and key technical notes: - Invoker contract: accept an event stream of typed frames {type, id?, payload}. We recommend a compact frame vocabulary: "token", "tool_call", "tool_result", "structured_fragment", "done", "error". - Progressive structured outputs: prefer emitting JSON fragments under "structured_fragment" that can be assembled incrementally into a deterministic array/object; emit a final checksum or version marker on "done". - Backpressure and cancelation: clients MUST expose pause/resume hooks for streaming parsers and provide a cancel token that routes to server-side connection termination. Example patterns below show a small state-machine that converts envelopes to a typed generator/async-iterator. - Tool/function call handshake: server emits "tool_call" with name + input; client returns a single "tool_result" frame per call (idempotency key recommended). If the client cannot handle the tool, it may emit a "tool_failed" frame and server should continue with fallback text. - Language differences: a) TypeScript: prefer AsyncGenerator<T> wrappers and explicit types for frames; use AbortController for cancellation. b) Python: use async iterators and explicit pydantic-like parse/validate step per fragment. c) Go: provide a channel-based iterator and a small Framer that yields events with minimal allocations. Examples (trimmed) for inclusion in the playbook: - TypeScript (recommended pattern): ```ts
// Example: streamToAsyncGenerator(fs) consumes fetch/Response.body as text/bytes
async function* parseStreamToEvents(stream: ReadableStream<Uint8Array>, signal?: AbortSignal) {
  const reader = stream.getReader();
  let buffer = '';
  while (true) {
    if (signal?.aborted) throw new Error('aborted');
    const {done, value} = await reader.read();
    if (done) break;
    buffer += new TextDecoder().decode(value, {stream: true});
    let idx;
    while ((idx = buffer.indexOf('\n')) >= 0) {
      const raw = buffer.slice(0, idx).trim();
      buffer = buffer.slice(idx + 1);
      if (!raw) continue;
      try { yield JSON.parse(raw); } catch (e) { yield {type: 'token', payload: raw}; }
    }
  }
}
``` - Python (recommended pattern): ```py
# Example: async parser that yields event dicts
async def parse_stream(aiter):
    buffer = ''
    async for chunk in aiter:
        buffer += chunk.decode('utf-8')
        while '\n' in buffer:
            line, buffer = buffer.split('\n', 1)
            line = line.strip()
            if not line: continue
            try:
                yield json.loads(line)
            except Exception:
                yield {'type': 'token', 'payload': line}
``` - Go (recommended pattern): ```go
// Framers should be simple and avoid allocations in hot path
func ParseLines(r io.Reader, out chan<- Event) error {
  scanner := bufio.NewScanner(r)
  for scanner.Scan() {
    line := strings.TrimSpace(scanner.Text())
    if line == "" { continue }
    var e Event
    if err := json.Unmarshal([]byte(line), &e); err != nil {
      out <- Event{Type: "token", Payload: line}
    } else {
      out <- e
    }
  }
  close(out)
  return scanner.Err()
}
``` Operational notes and trade-offs: - Framing as newline-delimited JSON is robust across transports (HTTP/2, HTTP/1.1 chunking, websockets). It trades off higher verbosity for simplicity and easier recovery. - Binary framing (length-prefix) is tighter but adds parsing complexity in language bindings; consider for future v3. - For structured outputs that are large, recommend chunked "structured_fragment" events where each fragment includes a path and a partial value so clients can merge incrementally. - For tool calls, include a required "id" field to correlate call/result pairs and to enable retries. Review/feedback timeline and outstanding questions: - 2025-02-18: Initial draft posted to Confluence: https://confluence.redwood.ai/display/SDK/Invoker+Playbook (WIP). - 2025-02-20: Engineering review with SDK leads (notes linked): https://drive.redwood.ai/drive/folders/1-InvokerReview-20250220 - Feedback: Design requested clearer UX for partial structured arrays (proposal: fragments include index ranges). - Feedback: Platform requested explicit guidance on CPU/memory trade-offs for client-side incremental validators. Action items: - Diego: produce final TypeScript example and CI test harness (due 2025-02-28). - Ava: coordinate Python and Go examples and add behavioral test cases to integration harness (due 2025-03-07). - PM review: schedule review with Product (Samir Patel) to confirm release notes and breaking-changes text (by 2025-03-05). Links to artifacts and PRs: - Draft playbook: https://confluence.redwood.ai/display/SDK/Invoker+Playbook - Example branch: https://github.com/redwood-inference/sdk-examples/tree/feature/interop-invoker - PR for TS glue: https://github.com/redwood-inference/sdk-examples/pull/482 - PR for Python parser: https://github.com/redwood-inference/sdk-examples/pull/485 - Integration tests (CI): https://github.com/redwood-inference/integration-harness/pull/212
